{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773449684,"nonce":"GUETEd0L2VP8LcFQLpSD2qtf2eY=","title":"Inbox acknowledgements are not scoped to the requesting session/branch","message":"When multiple agent sessions operate in the same working directory (or same repo), inbox acknowledgements are global — any session can acknowledge any item, regardless of which session submitted the originating auth request or continuation.\n\n**Scenario:**\n1. Session A (on branch `feature/x`) submits an auth request. Auth completes. Auth result lands in inbox.\n2. Session B starts in the same directory (different branch or same branch, different task). It sees the auth result in the inbox, assumes it's relevant context, and acknowledges it.\n3. Session A resumes. The auth result is gone — acknowledged by B. Session A never sees its own merge result.\n\n**Impact:**\n- The originating agent loses its auth result (success/failure, post-merge notes, linked issues).\n- Continuations intended for a specific branch can be consumed by unrelated sessions.\n- The inbox loses its reliability as a delivery mechanism for session-specific information.\n\n**Scope of the problem:**\n- Auth results should be scoped to the session (or branch) that submitted the request.\n- Continuations should be scoped to the branch they were written for.\n- A session in a different branch or with a different session ID should not be able to acknowledge items it did not originate or request.\n\n**Proposed fix directions:**\n1. Add a `target_session_id` or `target_branch` column to inbox items; only sessions matching the target can acknowledge.\n2. Scope the `mandible inbox ack` CLI to the current `$MANDIBLE_SESSION_ID` — reject acknowledgement if the item's target doesn't match.\n3. The session-start hook should only surface inbox items matching the current branch (already partially the case via `?branch=\u003cb\u003e` query param) — but acknowledgement must also be gated, not just display.\n4. Consider a \"soft ack\" model: mark items as acknowledged-by-session-X but keep them visible to other sessions until they explicitly ack or the item expires.","files":null}]}